    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 291129\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n429 AM MST Tue Sep 29 2026\n\n.UPDATE...12Z Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Bands of light to moderate rain with isolated thunderstorms will\n  continue through the rest of this morning for much of Arizona\n  before gradually shifting more over eastern Arizona this\n  afternoon.\n  \n- Flood Watches are in effect through early evening for most of\n  Arizona with the peak of the heaviest rainfall occurring this\n  morning.\n  \n- Below normal temperatures are expected to persist across much of\n  Arizona through the rest of the work week, while near normal\n  readings return to the western lower deserts starting \n  Wednesday.\n\n- Dry conditions will prevail from Thursday through early next\n  week as high pressure and gradual warming takes hold across the\n  region.\n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY AND TONIGHT/... \nActive weather will continue for at least the first half of today\nas an unseasonably strong Pacific low pressure system moves \neastward through the Desert Southwest. The low center is now \nlocated across southern California prompting strong forced ascent \nthrough much of Arizona. Lower level moisture advection yesterday \npushed mixing ratios upwards of 10-12 g/kg with the best moisture \nacross far eastern Arizona. However, drier air aloft has also \nbegun to work its way in from the southwest as it wraps in around \nthe low center. This drier air may limit rainfall rates some, but \nall in all will not prevent much of the area from receiving decent\nrainfall through the rest of this morning. \n\nCurrent objective analysis shows the best moisture pooling out\nahead of the low with PWATs of around 1.5\" across far south-\ncentral Arizona. Modestly strong mid-level southwesterly flow will\ncontinue to provide upslope ascent ahead of the low, while forced\nascent aloft, which is currently maximized over southwest \nArizona, gradually shifts eastward through this afternoon. \nScattered showers and thunderstorms have developed across southern\nand central Arizona since midnight with rainfall rates close to \ntwo inches per hour for the stronger convective cells. However, \ndue to a 30-35 kt steering flow, the storm residence time over one\nlocation will not be very long and training of cells will be \nneeded for heavy rainfall amounts. A few of the thunderstorms are \nalso becoming more discrete and potentially strong enough to bring\nstrong winds to the surface. Although we can't rule out strong to\nnear severe winds with the strongest storms, it is quite unlikely\ndue to the very limited (250-500 J/kg) CAPE. The biggest threat \nfor the rest of this morning will continue to be periods of \nmoderate to heavy rainfall with training of storms bringing \nlocalized rainfall amounts of 1-2\" of over a few hour period. \nFlood Watches are now in effect for much of south-central and \neastern Arizona and last through early evening. \n\nCAMs show these scattered showers and thunderstorms eventually\nforming a somewhat broken line by sunrise as they slowly\ntransition from west to east. The peak of the rainfall for the\nPhoenix metro is likely to fall between 4 AM and 8 AM this morning\nbefore shifting mostly east of the Phoenix area later this\nmorning. The highest rainfall amounts are expected to be focused \nacross the higher terrain northeast and east of Phoenix from \naround sunrise through noon. The band of showers and thunderstorms\nis likely to fill in more by around sunrise as it begins to move\ninto eastern Arizona before winding down late morning into the \nearly afternoon. The threat for flash flooding will also peak \nlater this morning with the bulk of shower and thunderstorm \nactivity shifting out of our area this afternoon.\n\nAs the low center moves into central Arizona this afternoon and \nevening, we are likely to have additional wrap-around showers and\nthunderstorms but mostly focused over the higher terrain between \nFlagstaff and Phoenix. We should have a few of these storms move \ninto northern La Paz, northern Maricopa, and eventually into Gila \nCounty this evening but CAMs mostly show isolated to at most \nscattered coverage. It seems likely the CAMs are underplaying this\npotential, so it would not be surprising to have another round of\nrainfall this evening across south-central Arizona. Lingering \nchances for some showers and maybe some embedded storms will \nremain possible over the eastern Arizona high terrain into the \novernight hours tonight before the low eventually ejects into New \nMexico by sunrise Wednesday morning. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nWednesday will be a transition day as the Pacific low gradually\nexits the region. Some morning scattered showers are likely to\nstill be across the far eastern Arizona high terrain, but any \nactivity should quickly wind down in the afternoon. Elsewhere on \nWednesday, sunny skies and warmer temperatures are forecast for \nsoutheast California and southwest Arizona while the south- \ncentral Arizona lower deserts will see some lingering clouds and \nwell below normal temperatures.\n\nModel guidance shows upper level ridging fully moving into our\nregion on Thursday and likely staying put through the upcoming\nweekend. This will lead to gradual drying and mostly clear skies\nwhile temperatures slowly rise closer to the normal range for the\nsouth-central Arizona lower deserts. The western lower deserts \nmay reach 100 degrees again starting Friday, but for the eastern \nhalf of Arizona some reinforcing cooler air from the east Friday \nthrough Saturday should keep daytime highs at or just above 90 \ndegrees through Saturday. This cooler easterly flow may even \ncontinue into early next week with temperatures in the Phoenix \narea staying right near seasonal normals for early October.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1130Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nThe main weather concerns through this morning will include\nperiods of SHRA/TSRA, lower CIGs, and erratic wind directions.\nLatest area radars indicate a batch of moderate to heavy SHRA/TSRA\nprogressing ewd into the Phoenix Metro. This band will likely last\nthrough 15Z-16Z before clearing east of all TAF sites. Expect\nMVFR/IFR vsby with cigs falling to around 4-5 kft AGL in the\nheaviest showers this morning with gusty N-NW winds developing for\na brief period before switching back out of the E/NE as SHRA \nmoves east of terminals during the mid-morning. Guidance suggests \ndirections quickly shifting to westerly by early this afternoon. \nWe will likely hold onto 5 kft BKN cigs through this evening. \nAfter sunset, lower cigs will dissipate while winds weaken, then \nobtain the traditional nocturnal east component by 03Z-04Z. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nThe main aviation weather concerns through the forecast period\nwill be gusty NW winds and potentially another batch of SHRA/VCSH\nat KBLH this afternoon. Rain has cleared east of the region,\nhowever a few showers will be possible later this afternoon on the\nback side of a departing weather system. These showers will likely\nbe brief and may impact KBLH. Winds at both terminals will veer\nout of the W-NW and become gusty throughout the day. Periodic\ngusts around 25 kts will be possible at both terminals before\nwinds subside after sunset.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nWidespread showers and isolated thunderstorms will affect much of\nthe area early today before mainly affecting the Arizona high\nterrain this afternoon and evening. Moderate to heavy rainfall is\nlikely to impact the Arizona high terrain through at least early\nafternoon. Humidities will begin to trend lower across the western\ndistricts later today, but more so on Wednesday. Elevated\nhumidities will persist across the eastern districts through at\nleast Thursday. Winds will be breezy to locally windy at times \ntoday before becoming light again on Wednesday. Drier conditions \nand near to slightly above normal temperatures are forecast for \nlater this week and through the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Flood Watch through this evening for AZZ534-537>563.\n\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...Salerno\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
